<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-undetermined-part-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Guess That Works</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO PROSE: CONTINUE FROM LAST PAGE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    We continue with the example from <xref ref="sec-undetermined-part-1"/>.
    Recall we were trying to find a particular solution to
    <me>3y'' + 4y' - 2y = 2\cos(t).</me>
    We tried the guess <m>y_p = A\cos(t)</m>, but it failed: the <m>y'</m> term
    introduced a <m>\sin(t)</m> that had nowhere to cancel. We need a guess
    flexible enough to absorb both sine and cosine terms.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: THE FIXED GUESS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="example-undetermined-AB-guess">
    <title>Finding a Particular Solution with <m>y_p = A\cos(t) + B\sin(t)</m></title>

    <!-- ── Part (a): MC on the guess ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-undet2-guess">
      <title>Choosing the guess</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Our last guess <m>y_p = A\cos(t)</m> failed because differentiating it
          produced a <m>\sin(t)</m> term we couldn't cancel. What modification
          to the guess might give us the flexibility to handle that?
          (Give it a shot, we will see in the next part why the right answer works!)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="no">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(t) + B\sin(t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Good instinct! In the next part we will see that including both
              <m>\cos(t)</m> and <m>\sin(t)</m> in the guess gives us enough
              room to cancel the sine term that appears when we differentiate.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(t) + Bt</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A polynomial term like <m>Bt</m> would introduce powers of <m>t</m>
              that have no match on the right-hand side. We need something that
              stays in the world of sines and cosines.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = Ae^t\cos(t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              An exponential factor would survive differentiation and produce
              terms with <m>e^t</m> that can't match the right-hand side.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p = (A + Bt)\cos(t)</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              This would produce polynomial-times-trig terms after differentiation,
              which is more complicated than we need here. A simpler modification works.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <!-- ── Part (b): Plug in and solve for A and B ── -->

    <task xml:id="task-undet2-solve">
      <title>Plugging in and solving for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> (can skim/reference if needed)</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the guess <m>y_p = A\cos(t) + B\sin(t)</m>. Its derivatives are:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>y_p' = -A\sin(t) + B\cos(t)</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>y_p'' = -A\cos(t) - B\sin(t)</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          Substitute into <m>3y'' + 4y' - 2y = 2\cos(t)</m> and solve for
          <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> to find <m>y_p</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Substituting:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \underbrace{3(-A\cos(t) - B\sin(t))}_{3y''}
              \amp+ \underbrace{4(-A\sin(t) + B\cos(t))}_{4y'}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp+ \underbrace{(-2)(A\cos(t) + B\sin(t))}_{-2y}
              = 2\cos(t).
            </mrow>
          </md>
          Collecting the sine and cosine terms separately:
          <me>
            \underbrace{\textcolor{blue}{(-4A - 5B)\sin(t)}}_{\textcolor{blue}{\sin(t)\text{ terms}}}
            + \underbrace{\textcolor{red}{(-5A + 4B)\cos(t)}}_{\textcolor{red}{\cos(t)\text{ terms}}}
            = \underbrace{\textcolor{blue}{0 \cdot \sin(t)}}_{\textcolor{blue}{\sin(t)\text{ terms}}}
            + \underbrace{\textcolor{red}{2\cos(t)}}_{\textcolor{red}{\cos(t)\text{ terms}}}.
          </me>
          Since <m>\sin(t)</m> and <m>\cos(t)</m> are independent, their coefficients
          must match on both sides, giving the system:
          <md>
            <mrow>\textcolor{blue}{-4A - 5B} \amp\textcolor{blue}{= 0}</mrow>
            <mrow>\textcolor{red}{-5A + 4B} \amp\textcolor{red}{= 2}</mrow>
          </md>
          <solution>
            <title>Solving for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m></title>
            <p>
              From the first equation: <m>B = -\dfrac{4}{5}A</m>.
              Substituting into the second:
              <md>
                <mrow>-5A + 4\!\left(-\frac{4}{5}A\right) \amp= 2</mrow>
                <mrow>-5A - \frac{16}{5}A \amp= 2</mrow>
                <mrow>-\frac{41}{5}A \amp= 2</mrow>
                <mrow>A \amp= -\frac{10}{41}.</mrow>
              </md>
              Then <m>B = -\dfrac{4}{5}\cdot\left(-\dfrac{10}{41}\right) = \dfrac{8}{41}</m>.
            </p>
          </solution>
          So <m>A = -\dfrac{10}{41}</m> and <m>B = \dfrac{8}{41}</m>, giving the particular solution:
          <me>y_p = \underbrace{-\frac{10}{41}}_{A}\cos(t) + \underbrace{\frac{8}{41}}_{B}\sin(t).</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BRIDGE PROSE: WORKS IN MOST CASES, NOT ALL
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    The guess <m>y_p = A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)</m> worked here, and it will work
    in most cases. However, there is one special situation where this guess fails:
    when the guess itself looks like part of the homogeneous solution. We call
    this the <term>case of duplication</term>, and we will need a different guess
    there. That case is coming up on the next page.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION BLOCK: USEFUL STRATEGY
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <observation xml:id="obs-undetermined-strategy">
    <title>A Useful Strategy for Most Non-Homogeneous Equations</title>
    <p>
      To find a particular solution <m>y_p</m> to
      <me>ay'' + by' + cy = m_1\cos(kt) + m_2\sin(kt),</me>
      guess
      <me>y_p = A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt),</me>
      substitute into the ODE, match coefficients of <m>\sin(kt)</m> and
      <m>\cos(kt)</m> on both sides, and solve for <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
      This works in most cases (see note above about duplication).
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: WHERE DOES k COME FROM?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    One thing to be careful about: the <m>k</m> in the guess comes directly from
    the right-hand side of the ODE. For example, in
    <me>ay'' + by' + cy = m_1\cos\underbrace{(4t)}_{k=4} + m_2\sin\underbrace{(4t)}_{k=4},</me>
    whatever frequency appears in the non-homogeneous term <m>g(t)</m> is the same frequency you use in your guess
    <m>y_p = A\cos(kt)+B\sin(kt) </m>.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT: IDENTIFY THE GUESS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-undet-guess">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which of the following is the correct form of the guess <m>y_p</m>
        for finding a particular solution to
        <me>y'' + 3y' + 2y = 5\cos(4t)?</me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(4t) + B\sin(4t)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The right-hand side has frequency <m>k = 4</m>, so the
            guess uses <m>\cos(4t)</m> and <m>\sin(4t)</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(t) + B\sin(t)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Close in form, but the frequency is wrong. The right-hand side has
            <m>\cos(4t)</m>, so the guess should use frequency <m>k = 4</m>, not <m>k = 1</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(4t)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This was our first instinct on the previous page, and it ran into trouble
            when the <m>y'</m> term introduced a sine. We need both <m>\cos(4t)</m>
            and <m>\sin(4t)</m> in the guess.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>y_p = A\cos(2t) + B\sin(2t)</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The frequency <m>k = 2</m> doesn't match the right-hand side.
            The <m>2</m> here comes from the coefficient of <m>y</m> in the ODE,
            not from <m>g(t)</m>. Always look at the right-hand side for <m>k</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>The strategy:</alert> To find a particular solution to
          <m>ay'' + by' + cy = m_1\cos(kt) + m_2\sin(kt)</m>,
          guess <m>y_p = A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)</m>, plug in, match
          coefficients of sine and cosine separately, and solve for
          <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Coming up:</alert> On the next page we will see the
          case of duplication, where the guess <m>y_p = A\cos(kt) + B\sin(kt)</m>
          fails and a different approach is needed.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

